  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is at the bottom of the ocean food web?",
      "a": "Phytoplankton: microscopic algae and bacteria that photosynthesise in sunlit water. They make the food that nearly every other sea creature depends on, directly or indirectly.",
      "asks": [
        "What do ocean food chains start with?",
        "Who are the producers in the sea?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How can the biggest whales live on tiny krill?",
      "a": "Krill swarm in enormous numbers, and a blue whale engulfs a huge mouthful of water full of them, then pushes the water out through its baleen plates, keeping the krill. Feeding low on the food web means lots of energy is available.",
      "asks": [
        "What do blue whales eat?",
        "How much krill does a whale eat?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are top predators?",
      "a": "Top predators, like orcas and great white sharks, are at the top of the food web and have few natural enemies. There are relatively few of them, and they help keep the numbers of other animals in balance.",
      "asks": [
        "What is an apex predator?",
        "What eats sharks?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens to dead things in the ocean?",
      "a": "Dead plankton and animals sink as a drizzle called marine snow, and bacteria break them down, releasing nutrients. A dead whale on the seabed, a whale fall, can feed deep-sea animals for decades.",
      "asks": [
        "What is marine snow?",
        "What is a whale fall?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are there fewer sharks than small fish?",
      "a": "Only about a tenth of the energy at one level reaches the next, because animals use most of it to live. So each level up can support far fewer animals than the one below.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are there so few top predators?",
        "What is the ten percent rule?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Roughly 10 percent of the energy at one level of a food chain passes to the next.",
    "A whale carcass on the deep seabed, called a whale fall, can feed deep-sea animals for decades."
